asyncr/----introduction----                       asyncr/----introduction----

This is a read-only fork of the original AsyncIO aka. "Fast AmigaDOS I/O".

This fork implements functionality similar to the original read mode of
AsyncIO, along with a subset of read features later added to asyncio.library.
But despite the similarities, the API and behavior of these functions differ
from the originals. Using this as a drop-in replacement may yield unexpected
results.

Some functionality needed for general-purpose use is still unimplemented.

This fork comes with no warranty.

asyncr/--historical background--             asyncr/--historical background--

Original introduction as written by Martin Taillefer.

Reading and writing data is crucial to most applications and is in many cases
a major bottleneck. Using AmigaDOS' sophisticated file system architecture
can help reduce, and sometimes eliminate, the time spent waiting for IO to
complete. This package offers a few small routines that can greatly improve
an application's IO performance.

Normally, an application processes a file in a manner similar to the following:

  1 - Open the file

  2 - Read some data

  3 - Process data just read

  4 - Repeat steps 2 and 3 until all data is processed

  5 - Close file

Although the above sequence works fine, it doesn't make full use of the Amiga's
multitasking abilities. Step 2 in the above can become a serious bottleneck.
Whenever the application needs some data by using the DOS Read() function,
AmigaDOS has to put that task to sleep, and initiate a request to the file
system to have it fetch the data. The file system then starts up the disk
hardware and reads the data. Once the data is read, the application is woken up
and can start processing the data just read.

The point to note in the above paragraph is that when the file system is
reading data from disk, the application is asleep. Wouldn't it be nice if the
application could keep running while data is being fetched for it?

Most Amiga hard drives make use of DMA (Direct Memory Access). DMA enables a
hard drive to transfer data to memory _at the same time_ as the CPU does some
work. This parallelism is what makes the set of accompanying routines so
efficient. They exploit the fact that data can be transfered to memory while
the application is busy processing other data.

Using the asynchronous IO routines, an application's IO happens like this:

  1 - Open the file, ask the file system to start reading ahead

  2 - Read some data, ask the file system to read more data

  3 - Process data

  4 - Repeat steps 2 and 3 until all data is processed

  5 - Close file

Immediately after opening the file, a request is sent to the file system to get
it reading data in the background. By the time the application gets around to
reading the first byte of data, it is likely already in memory. That means the
application doesn't need to wait and can start processing the data. As soon as
the application starts processing data from the file, a second request is sent
out to the file system to fill up a second buffer. Once the application is done
processing the first buffer, it starts processing the second one. When this
happens, the file system starts filling up the first buffer again with new
data. This process continues until all data has been read.

The whole technique is known as "double-buffered asynchronous IO" since it uses
two buffers, and happens in the background (asynchronously).

The set of functions presented below offers high-performance IO using the
technique described above. The interface is very similar to standard AmigaDOS
files. These routines enable full asynchronous read/write of any file.

asyncr/CloseAsyncR                                         asyncr/CloseAsyncR

   NAME
	CloseAsyncR -- close an async file.

   SYNOPSIS
	CloseAsyncR(file);

	void CloseAsyncR(struct AsyncRFile *);

   FUNCTION
	Closes a file. Once this call has been made, the file can no longer be
	accessed.

   INPUTS
	file - the file to close. May be NULL, in which case this function
	       is a no-op.

   SEE ALSO
	OpenAsyncR(), dos.library/Close()

asyncr/FGetsAsyncR                                         asyncr/FGetsAsyncR

   NAME
	FGetsAsyncR -- Reads a line from an async file (buffered). (V40)

   SYNOPSIS
	buffer = FGetsAsyncR( file, buffer, numBytes );

	STRPTR FGetsAsyncR( struct AsyncRFile *, STRPTR, ULONG );

   FUNCTION
	This routine reads a single line of text from the specified async file,
	stopping when a NEWLINE character or the End-Of-File (EOF) is reached.

	Up to (numBytes - 1) characters will be copied into the buffer. If
	characters are successfully read into the buffer, the resulting string
	is guaranteed to be null-terminated. If the line is terminated by a
	newline, the newline character will be included in the buffer.

	If a read error or an immediate EOF occurs before any characters are
	read, the function returns NULL and the buffer is left unmodified.

   INPUTS
	file     - Pointer to an AsyncRFile structure opened for reading.
	buffer   - Pointer to the memory area to receive the line.
	numBytes - The size of the destination buffer in bytes.

   RESULT
	buffer   - Pointer to the buffer passed in, or NULL on failure or
		   immediate EOF.
		   If NULL is returned due to an immediate EOF, IoErr() will
		   return 0. If NULL is returned due to a read error, IoErr()
		   will contain the relevant error code.

   NOTES
	This function is a wrapper around FGetsLenAsyncR() and closely mirrors
	the behavior of dos.library/FGets(), including V47+ bug-fixes, with
	the following predictable safety constraints:

	- If a mid-stream read error occurs after characters have already been
	  copied, the function returns NULL. However, the data already read
	  will be null-terminated in the buffer to prevent undefined behavior
	  if the buffer is accidentally read.

	- If 'numBytes' is 0, the function immediately returns NULL. IoErr()
	  will be set to 0, and no memory is modified. (Matches dos.library
	  V47.27 behavior).

	- If 'numBytes' is 1, the function immediately returns NULL. IoErr()
	  will be set to ERROR_LINE_TOO_LONG, and no memory is modified.

   SEE ALSO
	FGetsLenAsyncR(), dos.library/FGets()

asyncr/FGetsLenAsyncR                                     asyncr/FGetsLenAsyncR

   NAME
	FGetsLenAsyncR -- Reads a line from an async file with length. (V40)

   SYNOPSIS
	buffer = FGetsLenAsyncR( file, buffer, numBytes, len );

	STRPTR FGetsLenAsyncR( struct AsyncRFile *, STRPTR, ULONG, ULONG * );

   FUNCTION
	This routine reads a single line of text from the specified async file,
	stopping when a NEWLINE character or the End-Of-File (EOF) is reached.

	Up to (numBytes - 1) characters will be copied into the buffer. If
	characters are successfully read into the buffer, the resulting string
	is guaranteed to be null-terminated. If the line is terminated by a
	newline, the newline character will be included in the buffer.

	If a read error or an immediate EOF occurs before any characters are
	read, the function returns NULL and the buffer is left unmodified.

   INPUTS
	file     - Pointer to an AsyncRFile structure opened for reading.
	buffer   - Pointer to the memory area to receive the line.
	numBytes - The size of the destination buffer in bytes.
	len      - Optional pointer to a ULONG to store the number of characters
		   written to the buffer (excluding the terminating null byte).
		   Can be NULL if the length is not needed.

   RESULT
	buffer	 - Pointer to the buffer passed in, or NULL on failure or
		   immediate EOF.
		   If NULL is returned due to an immediate EOF, IoErr() will
		   return 0. If NULL is returned due to a read error, IoErr()
		   will contain the relevant error code.

   NOTES
	This function closely mirrors the behavior of dos.library/FGets(),
	including V47+ bug-fixes, with the following predictable behaviors
	designed to eliminate the standard FGets() blind-spots:

	- If a mid-stream read error occurs after characters have already been
	  copied, the function returns NULL. However, the data already read
	  will be null-terminated in the buffer, and 'len' (if provided) will
	  hold the exact count of those characters. This allows the caller to
	  recover partial data.

	- If 'len' is provided, it is ALWAYS updated. It will contain 0 if no
	  characters were read (due to immediate EOF, error, or invalid arguments),
	  or the valid character count if data was written.

	- If 'numBytes' is 0, the function immediately returns NULL. IoErr()
	  will be set to 0, no memory is modified, and 'len' (if provided)
	  is set to 0. (Matches dos.library V47.27 behavior).

	- If 'numBytes' is 1, the function immediately returns NULL. IoErr()
	  will be set to ERROR_LINE_TOO_LONG, no memory is modified, and 'len'
	  (if provided) is set to 0.

   SEE ALSO
	dos.library/FGets()

asyncr/OpenAsyncR                                             asyncr/OpenAsyncR

   NAME
	OpenAsyncR -- open a file for asynchronous IO.

   SYNOPSIS
	file = OpenAsyncR(fileName, bufferSize);

	struct AsyncRFile *OpenAsyncR(const STRPTR, ULONG);

   FUNCTION
	The named file is opened for reading and an async file handle
	returned.

	'fileName' is a filename and SHOULD NOT be a window specification such
	as CON: or RAW:, or "*". Using these functions with interactive handlers
	will only waste resources for no gain.

	'bufferSize' specifies the size of the IO buffer to use. There are
	in fact two buffers allocated, each of roughly (bufferSize/2) bytes
	in size. The actual buffer size use can vary as the size is rounded up
	to the double of filesystem blocksize to speed up DMA. And for files
	smaller than the rounded-up size the buffer allocation is capped at
	file size and degraded to single-buffer mode if the file is smaller
	than the filesystem blocksize.

	If the file cannot be opened for any reason, the value returned
	will be NULL, and a secondary error code will be available by
	calling the routine dos.library/IoErr().

    INPUTS
	name       - name of the file to open, cannot be a window specification
	bufferSize - size of IO buffer to use. 8192 is recommended as it
		     provides very good performance for relatively little
		     memory. If 'bufferSize' is 0 this function will return NULL
		     with IoErr() ERROR_LINE_TOO_LONG.

    RESULT
	file - an async file handle or NULL for failure. You should not access
	       the fields in the AsyncRFile structure, these are private to the
	       async IO routines. In case of failure, dos.library/IoErr() can
	       give more information.

    BUGS
        This function is also responsible for sending the read request for the
	initial buffer fill. If that read fails and the user cancels the retry
	requester, subsequent reads will return EOF for files that where
        degraded to single buffer mode.

    SEE ALSO
	CloseAsyncR(), dos.library/Open()

asyncr/ReadAsyncR                                             asyncr/ReadAsyncR

   NAME
	ReadAsyncR -- read bytes from an async file.

   SYNOPSIS
	actualLength = ReadAsyncR(file, buffer, numBytes);

	LONG ReadAsyncR(struct AsyncRFile *, APTR, LONG);

   FUNCTION
	This function reads bytes of information from an opened async file
	into the buffer given. 'numBytes' is the number of bytes to read from
	the file.

	The value returned is the length of the information actually read.
	So, when 'actualLength' is greater than zero, the value of
	'actualLength' is the the number of characters read. Usually
	ReadAsyncR() will try to fill up your buffer before returning. A value
	of zero means that end-of-file was immediately reached. Errors are
	indicated by a value of -1.

    INPUTS
	file     - opened file to read, as obtained from OpenAsyncR()
	buffer   - buffer where to put bytes read
	numBytes - number of bytes to read into buffer. If 'numBytes' is smaller
		   than 1 this function will return -1 with IoErr()
		   ERROR_LINE_TOO_LONG.

    RESULT
	actualLength - actual number of bytes read, or -1 if an error. In
		       case of error, dos.library/IoErr() can give more
		       information.

    SEE ALSO
	OpenAsyncR(), CloseAsyncR(), ReadCharAsyncR(), dos.library/Read()

asyncr/ReadCharAsyncR                                     asyncr/ReadCharAsyncR

   NAME
	ReadCharAsyncR -- read a single byte from an async file.

   SYNOPSIS
	byte = ReadCharAsyncR(file);

	LONG ReadCharAsyncR(struct AsyncRFile *);

   FUNCTION
	This function reads a single byte from an async file. The byte is
	returned, or -1 if there was an error reading, or if the end-of-file
	was reached.

   INPUTS
	file - opened file to read from, as obtained from OpenAsyncR()

   RESULT
	byte - the byte read, or -1 if no byte was read. In case of error,
	       dos.library/IoErr() can give more information. If IoErr()
	       returns 0, it means end-of-file was reached. Any other value
	       indicates an error.

   SEE ALSO
	OpenAsyncR(), CloseAsyncR(), ReadAsyncR(), dos.library/Read()

asyncr/ReadLineAsyncR                                     asyncr/ReadLineAsyncR

   NAME
	ReadLineAsyncR -- read a line from an async file.

   SYNOPSIS
	bytes = ReadLineAsyncR( file, buffer, numBytes );

	LONG ReadLineAsyncR( struct AsyncRFile *, STRPTR, LONG );

   FUNCTION
	This routine reads a single line of text from the specified async
	file, stopping when a NEWLINE character or the End-Of-File (EOF) is
	reached. Up to (numBytes - 1) characters will be copied into the
	buffer.

	Lines that are longer than 'numBytes' will be truncated. This routine
	will always try leave the read cursor at the beginning of the
	next line, or at EOF in case of the last line in the file.

	If a newline exists in the original line, it will be present in the
	truncated buffer.

	If any character is copied into the buffer and no error occurs, the
	resulting string is guaranteed to be null-terminated.

	The value returned is the length of the information actually read. So,
	when 'bytes' is greater than zero, the value of 'bytes' is the the
	number of characters read. ReadLineAsyncR() will try to fill up your
	buffer before returning. A value of zero means that end-of-file was
	immediately reached. Errors are indicated by a value of -1.

   INPUTS
	file     - opened file to read from, as obtained from OpenAsyncR()
	buffer   - buffer to read the line into.
	numBytes - The size of the destination buffer in bytes. If 'numBytes' is
		   smaller than 2 this function will return -1 with IoErr()
		   ERROR_LINE_TOO_LONG.

   RESULT
	bytes - number of bytes read. In case of error (-1 is returned)
	    dos.library/IoErr() can give more information. EOF is indicated
	    by a return of 0.

asyncr/SeekAsyncR                                             asyncr/SeekAsyncR

   NAME
	SeekAsyncR -- set the current position for reading or writing within
		     an async file.

   SYNOPSIS
	oldPosition = SeekAsyncR(file, position, mode);

	LONG SeekAsyncR(struct AsyncRFile *, LONG, AsyncRSeekModes);

   FUNCTION
	SeekAsyncR() sets the read cursor for the file 'file' to the position
	'position'. This position is used by the various read functions as the
	place to start reading. The result is the current absolute position in
	the file, or -1 if an error occurs, in which case dos.library/IoErr()
	can be used to find more information. 'mode' can be ASR_MODE_START,
	ASR_MODE_CURRENT or ASR_MODE_END. It is used to	specify the relative start
	position. For example, 20 from current is a position 20 bytes forward
	from current, -20 is 20 bytes back from current.

	To find out what the current position within a file is, simply seek
	zero from current.

    INPUTS
	file - an opened async file, as obtained from OpenAsyncR()
	position - the place where to move the read/write cursor
	mode - the mode for the position, one of ASR_MODE_START, ASR_MODE_CURRENT,
	       or ASR_MODE_END.

    RESULT
	oldPosition - the previous position of the read/write cursor, or -1
		      if an error occurs. In case of error, dos.library/IoErr()
		      can give more information.

    NOTES
	Seeking in empty files is not supported.

	Seeking to EOF is not supported.

	Seeking in files bigger than UINT_MAX is not supported, for such files
	it will seek to wrong position for 'position' > INT_MAX.

	Just like dos.library/Seek() the only reliable way to differentiate
	error from success is to check IoErr(). IoErr() is always set to 0 on
	success.

    BUGS
	A seek to a target outside of currently filled buffers will initiate a
	read packet to fill a buffer with the requested range. This read might
	fail even if the SeekAsyncR() call itself succeeds. The error will
	first show up on the the next read/seek call, and the read pointer
	will then be reset to the position it had before the seek. SeekAsyncR()
	will in such cases propagate the IoErr from the read packet in place
	of using ERROR_SEEK_ERROR. But for read functions the only way to
	detect this state is to call SeekAsyncR() with 'position' 0 and
	ASR_MODE_CURRENT, and compare the result to the result of the previous
	seek if a read directly after a seek fails.
	This is a limitation of the core architecture, and can not be fixed.

    SEE ALSO
	OpenAsyncR(), CloseAsyncR(), ReadAsyncR(), dos.library/Seek()
